<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Head to Toes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Identify keywords and phrases in texts. Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can locate and identify keywords related to body parts and clothing within sentences (e.g., identifying "head" and "hat" in a description). Can recognize simple story elements, such as characters and setting, in texts about body parts and clothing (e.g., identifying "the boy" and "his hat" in a story). Can identify the initial and final sounds with letter correspondence in body parts and clothing-related vocabulary (e.g., the /h/ and /d/ sounds and letters in "hand" or the /t/ and /z/ sounds in "toes" when shown images of a hand or toes).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Touch Your..." TPR activity. The teacher says "Touch your head!", "Touch your toes!", "Touch your hand!" and students follow the instructions. This quickly revisits key vocabulary from the previous lesson and gets students moving and engaged, connecting to the theme "Head to Toes!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of body parts (e.g., 'hand', 'hat', 'toes') and clothing items previously introduced. The teacher clearly pronounces each word, pointing to the word on the flashcard and the corresponding body part or realia (if available). For "hand" and "hat", the teacher explicitly emphasizes the initial /h/ sound. For "toes", the teacher emphasizes the initial /t/ sound. "This is my hand." "This is a hat." "These are my toes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a hand) "Is this a foot?" (Expected answer: "No, a hand.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a boy wearing a hat) "What is on his head?" (Expected answer: "A hat.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated story (narrative excerpt) displayed on a large chart, whiteboard, or projector. The story, titled "Leo Gets Ready," uses present simple tense and focuses on Leo identifying his body parts and putting on clothes. The text includes target vocabulary (head, hand, foot, leg, hat, shirt, pants, shoes) and grammar (This is..., He wears..., Put on...).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing items in a simple narrative text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each word and picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Who is in the story?" (Expected: "Leo.") "What is the story about?" (Expected: "Leo and his clothes / body parts.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher re-reads specific sentences. Students point to the corresponding body part or clothing item in their own bodies or on the picture. For example, "This is Leo's head." (Students touch their head.) "He wears a hat." (Students point to the hat in the picture.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Where is Leo's hat?" (Students point to the hat in the picture). "Where are Leo's shoes?" (Students point to the shoes in the picture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying keywords related to body parts and clothing. Phonemic Awareness: Identifying initial and final sounds with letter correspondence for words like "head," "hand," "toes," "hat." Grammar: Present simple ("This is..."), Imperative ("Put on...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Keyword Identification):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with short sentences taken from "Leo Gets Ready" and corresponding pictures. Students read each sentence (e.g., "This is his head.") and circle the keyword (e.g., "head"). For specific words like "hand" and "toes," students also underline the first and last letters and try to make the sounds. (e.g., H-a-n-D, T-o-e-S).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides another worksheet with incomplete sentences and a word bank of target vocabulary (head, hat, shoes, hand, leg, shirt). Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from the word bank to complete them. For example: "Leo wears a blue _____." (Word bank: hat, head, hand).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Reading & Matching):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with pictures of body parts/clothing and separate flashcards with simple phrases (e.g., "This is my arm," "Put on your shirt," "These are my toes"). One student reads a phrase, and their partner finds and shows the matching picture card. They check each other's reading and matching.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Illustration Identification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a large-print, simple text (a short paragraph or a few sentences) about a different child describing their body and clothing, similar to "Leo Gets Ready," but with new characters (e.g., "Mia's Clothes," "Sam's Day"). The text will incorporate the target vocabulary and grammar. Each text will be accompanied by a blank outline of a character or a scene.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads their assigned text together, taking turns reading sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they read, they identify the main character and any clothing items or body parts mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading, students work together to draw or color the character/scene on their paper, adding the specific clothing items mentioned in the text (e.g., "Mia wears a green shirt and blue pants. She has two big feet."). They might also label specific body parts (head, hands) if the text describes them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing reading, providing help with vocabulary, and encouraging collaborative identification of keywords and story elements.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short individual task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students submit their illustrated texts from Stage 4. The teacher checks if the drawings accurately reflect the clothing and body parts described in their text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Individual Task: The teacher provides a final short reading passage (1-2 sentences) about a body part or clothing item. Students read the passage and then draw the described item or circle the correct picture from a choice of three. (e.g., "This is on your foot." - Students circle a "sock" or "shoe" picture).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to locate and identify keywords related to body parts and clothing in texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to recognize simple story elements (character, described items).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of initial/final sounds and letters in target words (e.g., 'h' in hat, 't' in toes) during verbal checks or labeling.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one body part you read about today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to read the story?" (Students circle a happy face or a neutral face).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you see today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part about reading the story?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visuals helped comprehension, group work was engaging).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., more practice with "Where is...?" questions, more guided sentence construction to prepare for speaking).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3, students will use the vocabulary and structures learned in reading to actively speak and interact with classmates about body parts and clothing, perhaps by asking "Where is your ____?" and responding "This is my ____."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>